Track 2: Khawuleza (Boil the Bilge)
- Story & Context: Written as a tribute to the roof-top scavengers who spent their days diving into the submerged sub-basements for diesel fuel, this track centers on a tense, syncopated duel between an unamplified resonator guitar and a brutal, wood-cracking log drum pulse. Recorded live during a midnight generator collapse, the song relies on hand-claps and the metallic rattling of water pipes to keep the rhythm going while Silas improvised a haunting story about a diver who stayed down too long. The atmosphere is uncomfortably intimate, carrying the smell of wet wool and cheap gin through its dry, un-reverberated vocal takes. "You dig too deep in the mud, you bring up things that can't breathe," whispers a chorus of exhausted ravers over a pitch-bent sub-bass drop that shatters glass.
